Statement 1: In C++, a constructor is a special
function that is invoked automatically upon the
creation of a class object. So statement A is
correct.
The constructor name should be the same as the
class name, enclosed in parenthesis ():
class MyClass {     // The class
public:           // Access specifier
MyClass() {     // Constructor
cout << "Hello World!";
}
};
int main() {
MyClass myObj;    // Create an object of
MyClass (this will call the constructor)
return 0;
}
Statement 2: Functions that don't return a value
are known as constructors. Constructors,
destructors, and void functions make up the
family of functions that don't return a value. So
statement B is correct.
Statement 3: When inheritance occurs, the
derived class inherits all of the parent class's
members (fields, methods, etc.), but it is not
allowed to inherit the base class's constructor
since constructors are not considered class
members. It is only permitted to call the
constructor of the base class; the derived class is
not permitted to inherit constructors. So
statement C is correct.
class Base
{
public:
Base(char* name)
{
// ...
}
};
class Derived : Base
{
public:
Derived() : Base("hello")  // calling the
constructor of base class
{
// ...
}
};
Statement 4: We cannot declare a virtual
constructor. We should specify the exact type of
the object at compile time so that the compiler
can allocate memory for that specific type. So
statement D is not correct.
Therefore B is the correct answer.
